<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Djinguereber Mosque</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Djingareyber Mosque</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Djinguereber Mosque is the great mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, a landmark of earthen Sudano-Sahelian architecture. Tradition credits its building to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> after his return from pilgrimage in the 1320s, to designs by the Andalusi poet-architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ishaq-al-sahili">Abu Ishaq al-Sahili</a>, and it was rebuilt and enlarged in the 16th century. It forms part of the Timbuktu World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque.jpg" alt="Djinguereber Mosque" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KaTeznik · CC BY-SA 2.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djingareiber_cour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
